Given an equilibrium state $\bm U^{\text{eq}}$ and the corresponding equilibrium variables $\bm E^{\text{eq}}$, we have that the semi-discretization in \eqref{e:fd_semi_disc} is WB, and thus ${\boldsymbol{{\mathcal{K}}}}_{j+\frac12}={\boldsymbol{{\mathcal{K}}}}_{j-\frac12}={\boldsymbol{{\mathcal{K}}}}^{\text{eq}}$ for all $j$.
	Now, let us prove that, for one mP-Euler time step \eqref{e:mP}, the equilibrium state is preserved at the next time step. The extension to mPDeC is straightforward.
	Since ${\boldsymbol{{\mathcal{K}}}}_{j+\frac12}={\boldsymbol{{\mathcal{K}}}}_{j-\frac12}$, suppose, w.l.o.g., that ${{\mathcal{K}}}^h_{j+\frac12}={{\mathcal{K}}}^h_{j-\frac12}>0$, where ${{\mathcal{K}}}^h_{j+\frac12}$ denotes the first component of ${\boldsymbol{{\mathcal{K}}}}_{j+\frac12}$, corresponding to the water height $h$, the variable we want to keep positive. 
	Then, by \eqref{e:pd_entries}, we have that $p_{j,j+1} = d_{j,j-1} = 0$ and $p_{j,j-1} = d_{j,j+1} = \frac{1}{\Delta x}{{\mathcal{K}}}^h_{j+\frac12}$. 
	Since, the mP-Euler system has a unique solution \cite{burchard2003}, we prove that $\bm U^{n+1} = \bm U^n = \bm U^{\text{eq}}$ is a solution of the mP-Euler system.
	Indeed, the update formula for the first component $h$ reads
	\begin{align*}
		h^{n+1}_j &= h^n_j + \Delta t\left(  
		 \underbrace{p_{j,j-1}(\bm U^n)}_{={{\mathcal{K}}}^{\text{eq},h}}\underbrace{\frac{h_{j-1}^{n+1}}{h_{j-1}^{n}}}_{=1}
		+\underbrace{p_{j,j-1}(\bm U^n)}_{=0} \underbrace{\frac{h_{j+1}^{n+1}}{h_{j+1}^{n}}}_{=1} 
		-\underbrace{d_{j,j+1}(\bm U^n)}_{={{\mathcal{K}}}^{\text{eq},h}} \underbrace{\frac{h_{j}^{n+1}}{h_{j}^{n}}}_{=1}  
		-\underbrace{d_{j,j-1}(\bm U^n)}_{=0} \underbrace{\frac{h_{j}^{n+1}}{h_{j}^{n}}}_{=1} \right)=h_j^n
	\end{align*}
	that is clearly satisfied by the equilibrium. The case with negative ${{\mathcal{K}}}^h_{j+\frac12}$ is analogous.
